+#sidebar("Design Notes")[
+#strong[Coverage.] Strong fits by Focus number. Bellator: 1--4, 6, 16, 17, 19, 21. Furcifer: 1, 3--8, 11, 13, 14, 20. Savant: 7, 9--12, 15, 18, 21, 22. Interlocutor: 2, 8, 10, 11, 13--16. Explorator: 1--3, 5, 6, 9, 12, 17--20. Adept: 7, 9, 10, 12, 21--23. Any: 23, 24. Off-type combinations are encouraged: an Interlocutor who Masters a Weapon is a duellist-diplomat; an Adept who Was Born Behind the Wheel is a witch with a haunted truck.
 
-Off-type combinations are encouraged: an #emph[Interlocutor who Masters a Weapon] is a duellist-diplomat; an #emph[Adept who Was Born Behind the Wheel] is a witch with a haunted truck.
+#strong[Watch in playtest.] The tag-cap exceptions (Masters a Weapon, Is More Than Human) at Formidable and Heroic Difficulty. Focus abilities that echo playbook abilities (Beast Lore and Monster Lore, Trap Sense and Escape Artist) don't stack on one character: take the better one. Stated Create discounts still stack to the minimum (Core Rules §5).
 
-=== 9.2 Things to Watch in Playtest
-- #strong[Tag-cap exceptions] (Masters a Weapon, Is More Than Human): #strong[resolved (v0.3)] --- both abilities now add #strong[+1 above the normal +2 cap (maximum +3 from tags)] instead of ignoring the cap outright. Re-test rolls at Formidable/Heroic Difficulty after this change to confirm it still feels earned rather than trivial.
-- #strong[Once-per-session spotlight benefits:] distinguish actual automatic-success entries from escape guarantees, contacts, disguises and setup benefits. Only an ability expressly replacing a roll uses Core Rules §10.5's automatic-success procedure: it applies to an otherwise permitted action, not a trumped action, an unsuitable approach or the whole conflict. It grants no extra turn, free script change or expanded stakes. No roll produces no dice-based critical or complication. If several players have spotlight abilities, a table-wide one-per-scene limit is a possible playtest variant, not an active restriction imposed by these notes.
-- #strong[The Reveal is not automatic success.] The current Solves the Unsolvable Tier 3a entry requires correctly naming the culprit in front of others, grants +4 starting Disposition to the user's side in conflicts against them, and says they get no compromise if they lose. It does not replace an action roll. Preserve the stated benefit and named compromise exception until explicitly reviewed; generic automatic-success or compromise summaries do not rewrite them. The scope of eligible conflicts and interpretation of the compromise clause need explicit replacement text before any changed ruling is adopted.
-- #strong[Overlap with playbook abilities:] a few Focus abilities echo playbook abilities (Beast Lore / Monster Lore, Trap Sense / Escape Artist). That\'s intended for off-type characters; for on-type characters, the two don\'t stack. Take the better one. This overlap guidance does not replace the core rule allowing explicit Create discounts to accumulate to the normal minimum cost (Core Rules §5).
-- #strong[★ distribution:] every Focus has exactly one ★ option, and it\'s never the Tier 1 ability, so you can always reach Tier 3 at full Refresh.
-- #strong[Timing and payments:] free or immediate wording must follow the ability's actual trigger and action requirements; it does not retroactively cancel independently resolving actions. Generic prevention, redirection and incidental clearing do not cancel injury-trade payments, running-dry statuses, agreed concession costs or other accepted payments (Core Rules §10.5, §10.7).
-- #strong[Gaps for v0.3:] a dedicated faith/priest Focus (non-Weird reading: \"Keeps the Faith\"), a scholar-of-war Focus, a merchant/trader Focus, and a performer/artist Focus.
+#strong[Gaps.] Planned Focuses: faith (Keeps the Faith), scholar of war, merchant, performer.
